; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The evidence pack: what one deployment hands over when it answers for a span of time rather than
; for one response. A receipt attests a response; a pack attests that the responses inside a window
; are all of them, and it carries the two chain endpoints that let a reader with no network and none
; of our code establish that for themselves.
;
; `packages/receipt/src/pack.ts` writes these bytes as well as reading them, and its `signPack` will not sign a
; manifest its own reader refuses. What decides which receipts a span holds, and which pieces of the appraisal
; context those receipts' anchors name, is not here: the items, the two endpoints, the duty block, the references
; and any material a deployment attaches arrive from the deployment, which is the part that takes maintenance. That order
; is deliberate. A third party's verdict is read out of this container, so the container is public whether or
; not the tool that fills it is.
;
; Section 5.2 of `docs/receipt-spec.md` states the walk this container is built for and publishes the
; frames as byte images in `packages/fixtures/data/chain-v1.json`. Where a sentence here and those
; two disagree about a frame, they are the authority and this file is wrong.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ashaveri-Pack = #6.18(COSE_Sign1-Pack-COSE)

; The envelope is the receipt's, unchanged but for the content type: COSE_Sign1 per RFC 9052 section
; 4.2, encoded per RFC 8949 with Core Deterministic Encoding, so a stranger who rebuilds a pack from
; this file and the receipts inside it gets the same bytes back out and signs, or refuses, the
; document the issuer made.
COSE_Sign1-Pack-COSE = [
    protected: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Pack-Protected-Header,
    unprotected: { * any => any }, ; the map that carries no claim; see the note below the manifest
    payload: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Pack-Manifest,
    signature: bstr .size 64
]

; The same three labels the receipt signs with, and a typ that differs because the two documents come
; from one key: a reader who took a pack for a receipt would verify a signature over a manifest and
; report it as an attestation about a single response, which is the one mistake a content type exists
; to prevent and it is not recoverable afterwards, because both documents pass.
Ashaveri-Pack-Protected-Header = {
    1: -8,                    ; alg: EdDSA (Ed25519)
    4: bstr .size 32,         ; kid: sha256(public_key)
    3: "ashaveri/pack",       ; typ
}

; The signed manifest, and the only thing here a verifier of a pack has to believe before it starts
; walking. Each member passed one test: a reader of the pack needs it, and no receipt inside the
; container carries it. That test is why `span`, `chain` and `duty` are here and why the store's window
; counts, its policy and its retirement history are not; those describe the deployment's retention
; state, which a retention artifact states and this container carries nothing of. A second copy of a
; number the deployment already publishes elsewhere is the thing that drifts.
;
; Two of the eight pass in a way the sentence does not fit, and it is worth saying which so that a reader
; looking for one shape finds what is actually there. `items` is the body the other seven make statements about,
; so the test says little of it. `v` is the one member no walk consumes: a version states which
; rules the bytes are read under, so a reader needs it before it can read a member of anything, and
; needing it ahead of the walk is as strong a claim on the manifest as needing it during one.
;
; `v: 1` is the only version this file states, and a version here says what the fields attest rather than
; which software wrote them, as it does in `receipt.cddl`. The manifest names eight members and `custody` is
; among them: a pack whose sealed receipts state `held` slots and which signs for nothing about them leaves every
; such slot weighable only against an endpoint the reader has to reach somewhere else, which is a different document
; from the one the auditor holds, so the member is required and an empty list is a statement rather than an omission.
; Two of the eight arrived in place of one of them rather than beside it: the member that stood seventh here answered
; for a held slot by carrying the document itself, and it is retired, so a `v: 1` manifest naming it is refused as a
; member this version does not define rather than read with it dropped. The eighth member is the byte arm, whose
; entries a deployment may fill with material it holds rights to redistribute and which no code a deployment runs
; fills; the offline proof attaches synthetic documents it generated itself, to show a reader telling a pair from a
; body alone, and no vendor's bytes pass through this repository in either direction. The arm stands where the
; retired member stood, and its absence would be the same silence the retired member's
; dropping would be. An earlier shape of this container named six and not the seventh; it was never delivered outside
; this estate and no bytes carrying it exist to be read, so it is not a version this file has to refuse and the number
; starts at one. Nothing about the duty mapping, the framing or the envelope is expected to change without one.
Ashaveri-Pack-Manifest = {
    v: 1,                     ; format version
    at: int,                  ; unix seconds, the instant assembly began
    span: PackSpan,
    chain: PackChain,
    duty: PackDuty,
    items: [+ PackItem],      ; at least one; the reason is below
    custody: [* PackCustody], ; the references the sealed receipts' `held` slots answer for, which may be nothing
    attached: [* PackAttached], ; the material a deployment attaches beside those references, which may be nothing
}

; Stamped before the reads rather than after them, because a slow store must not be able to date a
; pack later than the window it describes: `held` is measured against this instant and the span is
; served as of it, so a stamp taken afterwards would move the answer without moving the evidence. It is
; also the bound that makes the reads mean anything, and the rule is `at` never before `to`: a window
; whose end has not arrived is still receiving, and a deployment that began its reads inside the span it
; is answering for would sign a completeness nobody was in a position to have at that instant.
;
; The span is half-open (`from` included, `to` excluded) because that is the bound the store serves
; by: a receipt issued at exactly the end stamp belongs to the next pack, not this one, and a format
; that left that out would let two packs both claim it or neither. A reader needs both bounds and not
; only the walk. The walk shows that nothing between the first item and the last is missing; the span
; is what shows the reader whether the run the walk covers is the period they asked for.
;
; Both bounds bind the items, which is a rule and not a tendency: every item's `iat` lies at or after
; `from` and strictly before `to`. The set that rule states is the set the store's own range query
; serves for the same two stamps, so a pack carrying a receipt stamped outside its span is two signed
; members contradicting each other, and a reader refuses it as a malformed document rather than reading
; it as a deployment that failed to hold what it answered for. That distinction is what this container
; exists to keep decidable: without it every inconvenient window arrives as a formatting error and
; every formatting error as a verdict about the deployment, which is the pair of mistakes a third party
; reads these bytes in order not to make.
PackSpan = {
    from: int,                ; unix seconds, included
    to: int,                  ; unix seconds, excluded
}

; The two endpoints section 5.2 names, both inside the signature. Neither is derivable from the items,
; which is the whole reason for carrying them: an anchor a reader invented would let a deployment start
; the walk wherever it liked, and a head read off the last item handed over would let it stop there,
; and a run of receipts the reader was never shown is exactly what a chain on its own cannot see.
; Publishing the head is what makes deletion detectable, and publishing it inside a signature is what
; makes it the deployment's own statement rather than the reader's guess.
;
; Before any retirement the anchor is thirty-two zero bytes, because the head of an empty chain has no
; predecessor; after one it is the `seam` the trim record carries. Both arrive here as the same value,
; and a reader does not have to know which happened in order to start walking.
PackChain = {
    anchor: bstr .size 32,    ; digest the first item was chained from
    head: bstr .size 32,      ; digest of the last item in the chain
}

; The duty this pack answers, and the revision of the mapping it was answered from.
;
; `rev` is the member that makes the other three readable later. The period a duty requires comes out
; of a mapping that changes, and a claim made in 2027 has to be read against the revision it was made
; from rather than against whatever the mapping says on the day somebody opens the artifact. Without
; `rev` a pack dates its own conclusion by its own opening, which is how a format silently rewrites
; the record it was written to hold.
;
; `required` is the deployment's to state and this format supplies no default for it. 19(1) has a floor
; the deployment measures against; 19(2) and 26(6) route a financial institution's logs into the
; applicable Union financial-services law, where the period is longer and is not ours to name. An
; artifact written against a routed article carries the period that law states, and a reader who cannot
; see where the number came from has `rev` to ask it of. `art` is a label from the retention-duty
; registry, which this estate reads as "19(1)", "19(2)" and "26(6)". It is a tstr and not an enum for
; the reason `mk.sch` is in `receipt.cddl`: the reader that knows the registry refuses a label outside
; it, and this file does not decide a question it cannot check.
;
; `held` is not derivable from the items either, which is the only reason it is stated here rather
; than left to a reader: a pack covers one span, the duty runs against everything the store still
; holds, and the oldest receipt in this container need not be the oldest one retained.
;
; What it does have to agree with is the container carrying it. `held` is a count of seconds measured at
; `at`, so it is at least the age of the oldest receipt in this pack (`at` less the earliest `iat`
; among the items, since a receipt handed over here is one the store still holds) and a smaller figure
; contradicts the pack the way the span bound above does, and is refused the same way. A `held` short of
; `required` is nothing of that kind: the document is whole, the arithmetic comes out the way it comes
; out, and the duty is not met. `rev` is never after `at`, because a mapping revision that lands after
; the reads began is not the revision the period was read from.
;
; `met` is absent on purpose. A reader holding `at`, `held` and `required` computes it in one
; comparison, and what a deployment signs about its own compliance is a conclusion where what it signs
; about the window is evidence. An artifact that states the conclusion as well invites a reader to
; stop checking the numbers.
PackDuty = {
    art: tstr,                ; a label from the retention-duty registry, e.g. "19(1)"
    rev: int,                 ; unix seconds, the revision `required` was read from
    required: int,            ; seconds that revision required
    held: int,                ; seconds the store has held the oldest receipt it retains, at `at`
}

; One reference to a piece of the appraisal context that a receipt inside this container names and a reader cannot
; otherwise reach: vendor-signed collateral, a validity window's material, revocation data. The entry states where
; the document was read from, what was asked of it, what it said it was, when its last byte landed, what its bytes
; and the header beside them hash to, which arm weighed them, and the window it signed for itself. It states no
; verdict, because whether those bytes verify is settled by the roots a reader stands behind and at an instant the
; reader chooses, and a signed record that printed the conclusion would turn custody of bytes into verification of
; them; and it states no content, because the bytes a vendor's service produced are that service's Output, and the
; terms of the endpoints in `packages/collateral/src/intel-origin.ts` license an attestation service to hold them
; and not this estate to hand them on.
;
; Resolution is by the slot a sealed receipt names and by nothing else. The entry's `k` is that slot: the id the pack
; gives the record and which half of its anchor the reference answers for. A reference keyed by anything the issuer
; chose would be a second name for one digest, and a second name is the thing that drifts; a reference that named no
; held slot would be a record of an observation nobody asked it to stand behind. Both directions are refusals.
;
; The list may be empty. Every sealed receipt states an anchor with both of its halves, so the pack that refers to
; nothing is the one whose every slot states an absence; the member is still there and still says so, because an
; absent member and an empty list are two different claims and a closed map that let the first pass would make the
; reader guess which one it was holding.
;
; `a` and `c` are what a reader needs in order to decide whether a chain has to arrive beside a body at all, and they
; are the reason a reference is weighable where a run of bytes is not. `a` names the arm that weighed the document at
; capture: `served` for one whose issuer chain arrived in a header beside the body, `embedded` for one whose
; certificates sit inside it. `c` is the digest of that header exactly as it arrived, or null where none did. A
; chain's own bytes are no member of this container, which is why `c` is a digest rather than a second run of bytes:
; the header is the vendor's response, and a reference states what it hashed to without distributing it. A reader
; holding the body and the header can recompute `b` and `c` and weigh the pair; a reader holding neither is told what
; was seen, by which arm, and when, and reaches nothing.
;
; The instants are checked before any of the material is believed. `s` and both ends of `w` are a whole number of
; Unix seconds inside the band the receipt format already states, which is the rule that keeps a millisecond clock
; from being read as a second one; the ends are named in the refusal for the same reason the receipt's windows give
; them.
;
; Two ceilings, each taken from a number this container already states rather than one this block invented. The
; count of references is capped at two per item, which is the count of slots the receipts this pack seals can name,
; `cva.col` and `cva.val`; a longer list cannot be all answers, and an entry named by nothing is already a refusal,
; so the bound the pack's own contents state is the bound a reader can check without a second source. A request's
; text is capped at 2048 bytes, which is the figure this file states rather than a number in two places. Both are
; computable from members a reader has already read, which is the point: a container whose size is bounded only by
; its issuer's patience is not an evidence document, it is a storage and parse surface that somebody signed.
;
; What a reader refuses here, in the order it is reached, and each refusal names its position. An entry that answers
; for no held slot. A held slot no entry answers for. An instant that is not a whole number of Unix seconds inside
; the band. A list longer than the two slots per item allows.
PackCustody = {
    k: PackCustodyKey,        ; which sealed receipt and which half of its anchor
    o: tstr,                  ; the origin declaration this read, by its declared name
    u: tstr .size (1..2048),  ; the request as asked, path and query members included
    i: null / tstr,           ; the identity the document named for itself, or null
    s: int,                   ; unix seconds, the instant the last byte landed
    n: null / int,            ; what that source declares about itself, or null
    b: bstr .size 32,         ; sha256 of the body exactly as it arrived
    c: null / bstr .size 32,  ; sha256 of the chain header exactly as it arrived
    a: "served" / "embedded", ; which arm weighed it at capture
    w: PackWindow,            ; the window the document stated for itself
    y: tstr,                  ; the cache key, spelled from the declared key members
}

; The slot one reference answers for, stated as the pack names the record and as the receipt names the half of its
; anchor. Both are needed and neither is derivable from the other: the digest a slot states is what the reference is
; about, and the pair here is what says which slot said it.
PackCustodyKey = {
    item: tstr .size (1..65535), ; the id the pack gives the record
    slot: "col" / "val",         ; which half of that receipt's anchor
}

; The two ends of the window a document signed for itself, in the order the document states them: when it began to
; stand and when it stops being read as standing. Both are the vendor's own signed statement, carried here as the
; instants a reader weighs a held slot against, and neither is a verdict about whether the statement is true.
PackWindow = {
    from: int,                ; unix seconds, the instant the document says it began to stand
    to: int,                  ; unix seconds, the instant it says it stops being read as standing
}

; The byte arm: the material itself, whole, for the one case the terms of the services allow, which is a deployment
; holding rights to hand those particular bytes on. The layout permits it and this repository's writers never fill it;
; what decides whether a deployment may attach what it holds is its own agreement with the service that answered, and
; nothing here speaks to that question.
;
; An entry resolves to exactly one reference, by the digest its own bytes hash to beside that reference's `b`. Each
; digest is recomputed by the reader rather than believed, and a statement that disagrees with the bytes beside it is
; a refusal rather than a second opinion. A chain's presence is paired with the digest stated for it: a body arriving
; with a header no reference names a digest for is a header nothing in the signed document says anything about.
;
; One ceiling, taken from the same place the format has always taken it: an entry's bytes are capped at 65535, which
; is the largest byte count a pack already bounds, `PackItem.id`, because the framing spends two bytes writing an
; id's length. Inheriting the figure is what keeps the bound the format's rather than the issuer's.
PackAttached = {
    bytes: bstr .size (1..65535),      ; the material, whole, when a deployment attaches it
    sha256: bstr .size 32,             ; sha256 of those bytes
    chain: null / bstr .size (1..65535), ; the header that arrived beside them
    chain_sha256: null / bstr .size 32,  ; sha256 of that header
}

; One receipt, with the three values its record was hashed with and nothing else. These four are the
; whole of what it takes to rebuild the frame: `kind` is 0 for every item, because a trim record
; carries no receipt and is never an item; `idLen` and `len` are counts of the bytes that follow them;
; and the digest the next item names (or the head, for the last one) is sha256 over
; 0x00 || prev || iat as eight big-endian bytes || the byte length of id as two big-endian bytes
; || id || receipt. Every integer in that input is unsigned and big-endian, which is the framing
; section 5.2 publishes, and the `id` bound below is that framing's own: `idLen` is two bytes, and an
; empty id belongs to a trim.
;
; The item's `iat` is the stamp the record was chained with, and it has to equal the `iat` the receipt
; itself attests. They are two statements and the store does not make them one: it chains a receipt
; under the stamp it was handed. A reader who checked the walk and not the equality would accept a
; receipt moved into a window it was never issued in, so the equality is part of the walk and no
; member here stands in for it.
;
; Order in the array bears nothing: the reader follows the `prev` links, because retiring a prefix and
; compacting a file rewrites the front of a chain without touching a surviving record's digest. The
; item whose `prev` is the anchor is first and the item whose own digest is the head is last. A dropped
; receipt breaks a link, and a receipt borrowed from elsewhere is either unreached or named by nobody's
; `prev`, so the chain sees both of those. It does not see the third case, and the format says that
; plainly rather than implying the opposite: an item parked beside a span it is not part of reaches the
; head exactly as the honest ones do, because the walk stops where the head is and never counts what it
; did not need to visit. So every item reached from the anchor, and exactly one ending the walk, is a
; rule a conforming reader enforces and this container cannot: the reader's check has two halves, the
; walk from the anchor to the head and the count of what that walk reached against the array it was
; handed, and the second is the only one with eyes for a parked receipt. A verifier that implements the
; first alone has implemented half a rule and will pass a pack the format refuses.
;
; No index travels with an item either: an ordinal would state a second time an order the bytes already
; fix, and would be free to disagree with it.
;
; The two orders a pack carries are the links and the stamps, and only one of them is the format's. The walk
; follows `prev`, which is the order the chain fixes; an item's `iat` is the stamp that record was chained
; under, and a store chains under whatever stamp it was handed when it appended, so a deployment that corrected
; its clock signs an honest pack whose stamps run backwards while every link in it is right. Nothing here
; refuses that document: refusing lawful output is as wrong as accepting a forgery, and no rule this file states
; is broken by a backwards stamp. A reader compares the two orders and reports a disagreement as a finding of
; its own, naming the step, which is the only reading both statements support.
;
; No two items in a pack carry the same `id`. The store serves one receipt per id, its index being a map
; from that name to a record, so a pack drawn from a range query cannot hold two and the rule is written
; for a reader rather than for a query. It is stated because a reader reports a failure by naming an
; item, and two items answering to one name make that report mean whichever of the two the reader
; happened to meet first: the links order the items and the name identifies them, and neither of those
; is a matter of arrival.
;
; `[+ …]` requires at least one item. A pack of nothing has no first node, so its walk is vacuous: the
; reader could check that the head equals the anchor and nothing else, and the artifact would read as
; evidence while proving only that somebody signed two digests. That a span held no receipts at all is
; a statement about the window, and it belongs to the artifact that states windows rather than to the
; container that carries receipts.
PackItem = {
    id: tstr .size (1..65535), ; the store's own id for the receipt, as the record names it
    iat: int,                 ; unix seconds, the stamp the record was chained at
    prev: bstr .size 32,      ; digest of the record before it, or the anchor for the first item
    receipt: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Receipt, ; the signed receipt bytes, whole and unaltered
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Closure is one rule across this file and not a rule about one map, which is how `receipt.cddl`
; states it and why this file may. No block above carries a `...`, and the absence means there what it
; means there: a member the version named by `v` does not define makes the document invalid rather
; than a document read with the unexpected member dropped. That reaches the manifest, the span, the
; chain, the duty, the item, the custody entry, its key, its window, the attached entry and the signed
; header alike. The header closes under the stronger of the
; two reasons. Its bytes are hashed into the `Sig_structure`, so a label that block does not name is
; an authenticated parameter, and a reader that took its three labels out of a header carrying a fourth
; would hand on a document other than the one the issuer signed. Its members are also the COSE
; registry's integers, which is why they are numbered rather than named.
;
; One map a signer fills at will, and that is a decision rather than a gap: the `unprotected` map of
; `COSE_Sign1-Pack-COSE` sits outside the signature, so nothing written in it can travel as a claim
; about the span, and enforcing that it is empty would buy strictness with no security content behind
; it. What the format declares of it is only that it carries no claim.
;
; Every number above written `int` is a CBOR integer, major type 0 or 1, and no other major type
; carries one. A floating-point number whose value happens to be a whole number is not an integer
; written awkwardly: it is a different type, and the only reader that can ever tell them apart is one
; reading the bytes, because a decoder that hands both over as one JavaScript number leaves a check
; placed afterwards nothing to distinguish. So a reader decodes every map this file names member by
; member (the manifest, each map the manifest opens, and the protected header) where no
; floating-point number may appear, in a value and in a key alike. Nothing any of them declares can be
; a float, so that rule refuses no document that follows this file. It matters here more than it might
; elsewhere, because the quantities a manifest states are durations and instants, and a duration is the
; kind of quantity somebody reaches for a fraction of, which is precisely the reading this rule is
; there to make impossible rather than merely unlikely.
;
; Sign is that rule's other half, and it belongs to a reader in the same way: every instant this file
; names is a unix time no earlier than the epoch and every duration a whole number of seconds no
; smaller than zero, so a negative `held` or an end stamp below the epoch is a malformed document rather
; than an unusual way of writing a quantity. CDDL writes both `int` because it owns no clock, and the
; floor belongs to the meaning rather than to the type.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
